    ":question/problem": "If $f(u)=\\begin{bmatrix}2u \\\\ {u}^{2} \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}$ and $u(t)=2t+2$, then $(f∘u)^{′}(t)=$",
    ":question/worked-solution": "Using the chain rule, we can write\n\n$$\n(f∘u)^{′}(t)={f}^{′}(u(t)){u}^{′}(t)\n$$\n\nLet's now find the derivatives of each function.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(u) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}(2u) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}\\,({u}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}(3)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2u \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {u}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t+2)=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(f∘u)^{′}(t) & =f′(u(t))u′(t) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2u(t) \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot {u}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2(2t+2) \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 8t+8 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"
  },
