    ":question/math-academy-id": "q-71222",
    ":question/problem": "If $f(u)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{sin}⁡u \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡u \\\\ {e}^{u}\\end{bmatrix}$ and $u(t)=4t-3,$ then $(f∘u)^{′}(t)=$",
    ":question/worked-solution": "Using the chain rule, we can write\n\n$$\n(f∘u)^{′}(t)={f}^{′}(u(t)){u}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nLet's now find the derivatives of each function.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(u) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡u) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}\\,(\\operatorname{cos}⁡u) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}({e}^{u})\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡u \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡u \\\\ {e}^{u}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {u}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4t-3)=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(f∘u)^{′}(t) & ={f}^{′}(u(t)){u}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u(t)) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u(t)) \\\\ {e}^{u(t)}\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot {u}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t-3) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t-3) \\\\ {e}^{4t-3}\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t-3) \\\\ -4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t-3) \\\\ 4{e}^{4t-3}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"
  },
