    ":question/math-academy-id": "q-128570",
    ":question/problem": "If $g(u)=⟨\\frac{1}{u},\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(u+1),\\,1⟩$ and $u(t)=\\frac{1}{t}$, then $(g∘u)^{′}(t)=$",
    ":question/worked-solution": "By the chain rule, we have \n\n$$\n(g∘u)^{′}(t)=g′(u(t))u′(t).\n$$\n\nNow, let's find the derivatives of each function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{′}(u) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}\\left(\\frac{1}{u}\\right),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(u+1)),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}(1)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}},\\,\\frac{1}{u+1},\\,0⟩ \\\\ {u}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(\\frac{1}{t}\\right)=-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(g∘u)^{′}(t) & =g′(u(t))u′(t) \\\\  & =⟨-\\frac{1}{(u(t))^{2}},\\,\\frac{1}{u(t)+1},\\,0⟩\\cdot {u}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =⟨-{t}^{2},\\,\\frac{t}{1+t},\\,0⟩\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}\\right) \\\\  & =⟨1,-\\frac{1}{t+{t}^{2}},0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"
  },
