    ":question/id": "c43762c8-310f-5f86-a903-48bba629edc4",
    ":question/math-academy-id": "q-70695",
    ":question/problem": "If $f(u)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡u \\\\ {u}^{3} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡u\\end{bmatrix}$ and $u(t)={e}^{t}$, then $(f∘u)^{′}(t)=$",
    ":question/worked-solution": "Using the chain rule, we can write\n\n$$\n(f∘u)^{′}(t)={f}^{′}(u(t)){u}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nLet's now find the derivatives of each function.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(u) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡u) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}\\,({u}^{3}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡u)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡u \\\\ 3{u}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{u}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {u}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t})={e}^{t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(f∘u)^{′}(t) & ={f}^{′}(u(t)){u}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u(t)) \\\\ 3(u(t))^{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{u(t)}\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot {u}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡({e}^{t}) \\\\ 3({e}^{t})^{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{{e}^{t}}\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot {e}^{t} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡({e}^{t}) \\\\ 3{e}^{2t} \\\\ \\frac{1}{{e}^{t}}\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot {e}^{t} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡({e}^{t}){e}^{t} \\\\ 3{e}^{3t} \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"
  }
